---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Simple from "../../layouts/Simple.astro";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles", ({ data }) => data.section === "compare")).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
---
<Simple
  title="Compare analytics tools"
  description="Side-by-side comparisons of analytics tools, including DataFast against Plausible, Fathom, Google Analytics, PostHog, Mixpanel, and Amplitude."
  kicker="Compare"
  lede="A comparison is a decision between two jobs. If the jobs differ, the honest result is often to keep both tools, or to keep the one you already run well."
  crumbs={[{ href: "/", label: "Home" }, { label: "Compare" }]}
>
  <p>PostHog and Google Analytics 4 overlap on pageviews and diverge on ads, warehouses, session replay, and feature flags. DataFast is a different comparison: revenue by channel, not a suite. The matchups below put datafa.st next to Plausible, Fathom, GA4, PostHog, Mixpanel, and Amplitude. Prices on those pages were re-read on 25 November 2026.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/reviews/datafast/">DataFast review</a> says what the product is, including the companies that are not it. The <a href="/pricing/datafast/">DataFast pricing</a> page holds the event ladder so the comparisons can stay about the job. The <a href="/alternatives/">alternatives</a> hub is the shortlist when you are leaving a tool rather than pairing two logos.</p>
  <section slot="wide" class="band band-yellow">
    <div class="article-wide">
      <h2>Comparisons</h2>
      <div class="guides">
        {articles.map((article) => (
          <a class="guide" href={`/${article.id}/`}>
            <span class="type">Compare</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{article.data.title}</h3>
              <p>{article.data.description}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="meta">{`/${article.id}/`}</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Simple>
